\documentclass{article}
\begin{document}
\section{Methods and Results}
We formalize query routing as a bandit problem. Each arm corresponds to a candidate index structure, and rewards are measured latencies. The explorer uses Thompson sampling with a Gaussian prior.
We now turn to the empirical evaluation of the proposed scheme.
On the TPC-H workload the router converges within 200 queries. Mean latency drops 31\% against the static baseline. Tail latency improves most on join-heavy queries.
